<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Hakam II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">915–976</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as al-Mustansir bi-llah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Hakam II was the second Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, reigning from 961 to 976 after his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a>. A scholar and bibliophile, he assembled a library said to hold some 400,000 volumes and enlarged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Great Mosque of Córdoba</a> with a richly decorated mihrab. His reign was peaceful and prosperous, but his young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a> fell under the control of the chamberlain al-Mansur.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii.jpg" alt="al-Hakam II" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Numismática Pliego · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlHakemII_dirhem_MedinaAzahara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Al-Hakam II was born on 13 January 915, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a> and Murjan, and succeeded his father in 961. He secured peace with the Catholic kingdoms of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and used the stability to build irrigation works, widen streets and build markets. Internal administration passed increasingly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> al-Mushafi, while the general Ghalib ibn Abd al-Rahman gained influence as leader of the army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. There he repulsed the last Norman attacks, about 970, and fought the Zirids and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, where the Fatimids were defeated in 974. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> kept its supremacy over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León.</p><p>From 961 al-Hakam enlarged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Great Mosque of Córdoba</a>, extending the prayer hall 45 metres to the south. The addition brought a richly decorated mihrab, intersecting multifoil arches and four ribbed domes, which survive today. The palace-city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madinat-al-zahra">Madinat al-Zahra</a>, begun by his father after 936, was also built up and renovated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Al-Hakam was well versed in many sciences and had books bought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>. His patronage brought him such fame that even books written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> were dedicated to him. A large translation effort rendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek works into Arabic, carried out by a joint committee of Muwallad Muslims and Mozarab Catholics. He wrote a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> himself.</p><p>The mathematician Lubna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> served as his private secretary, and the physician and surgeon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-zahrawi">Abu al-Qasim al-Zahrawi</a> was active at his court. After his death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> had the books of ancient science destroyed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Al-Hakam married Subh, a Basque concubine who held strong influence over the court, and they had two sons, Abd al-Rahman, who died young, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a>. A stroke near the end of his life curtailed his activities and may explain why he did not prepare his son for leadership. Modern scholars think a second stroke, possibly brought on by cold weather, killed him in October 976. Hisham II was eleven years old at his accession, and the regency was held by Ghalib, al-Mushafi and Subh, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> as her secretary.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Hakam II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jewish physician and diplomat Hasdai ibn Shaprut served Abd al-Rahman III and <b>al-Hakam II</b> and patronised a revival of Hebrew grammar and poetry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>On the death of his father Abd al-Rahman III, <b>al-Hakam II</b> became the second Umayyad caliph of Córdoba.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-15">15 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=954" aria-label="Córdoba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4431 801 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of al-Hakam II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4881 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4881 1071h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=954">What was here in 954 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
